Need for Speed: Most Wanted is the original game represented by this project.
Original game names, executable code, artwork, audio, tracks, cinematics and
other data retain their original ownership and notices. Original in-game credits
are preserved. This project is not affiliated with or endorsed by the original
game's publishers.

The configuration, tests, tools and documentation in this repository are covered
by LICENSE. The native host, compatibility runtime and translator are recomp-kit
by veritr1x, included in kit/ with this port's changes, under its own LICENSE and
NOTICE.

Original game files are not distributed in this repository and are not relicensed
by its MIT license. A translation of speed.exe is generated locally under
ignored build/ from the player's own copy by the kit's tools; nothing generated is tracked, and the
translation claims nothing over the original work's behaviour or ownership. The
game's executable, data, artwork, audio, tracks and cinematics are still required
from the player's own copy and are never included.

Ghidra, Capstone, pefile, Unicorn, NumPy and Pillow are external development
tools with their own licenses, not original game dependencies at runtime. The
kit's NOTICE lists the components it links (Lua, TinySoundFont, SDL3, volk, the
Vulkan headers) and their licenses.

Windows-only components a game folder may contain (an ASI loader such as dinput8.dll, scripts/ with ASI mods) are
not part of the port and are left out of every app the setup packages. They retain their own ownership and licenses.

The engine-rate and resolution patch points in mods/core/nfsmw (the
simulation frame time, its readers, and the resolution getters) follow the
analysis in ThirteenAG's WidescreenFixesPack, NFSMostWanted.WidescreenFix,
MIT License, Copyright (c) 2018 ThirteenAG. No code from it is included;
the addresses were checked against this executable.

Native Extra Options (weather and cinematic settings, Black Edition content, replayable Blacklist rivals, special
vinyls, the max-performance shop button, reward markers, longer profile names, starting cash, heat levels and the
helicopter takedown bounty) follow the behaviour of ExOptsTeam/NFSMWExOpts, commit
8a094fe29d28195bdcf69122dbfe945f16315f9a (GPL-3.0). They are reimplemented natively against verified PC binary
values; no code from it is included, and the Windows ASI and its instruction patches are not bundled.

The rear-view mirror improvements (HD resolution, full scenery, vehicle lights, props and shadows in the mirror)
restore what the community HD Reflections mod restores, reimplemented natively against the PC executable; no code
from it is included.

The texture-pack loader (mods/core/nfsmw/texture_pack.h) reads the TexWizard pack format; its engine entry points
were taken as leads from the TexWizard project (R-033/texwizard, MW_Address.h) and verified against this executable.
No code from it is included. Texture packs (for example the community "Need for Speed Most Wanted (2005) - Xbox 360
Stuff Pack") are the player's own downloads; none is included, and only their texture files are used.

Camera field names used by the depth of field and motion blur host inputs follow public reverse-engineering notes
(dbalatoni's Most Wanted camera structures) and were checked against the PC executable.

The rebuilt post-process effects (effects/clean-chain.hlsl) and light pools (effects/clean-pools.hlsl,
effects/lamp-classes.toml) were written for this port from a functional description of the standard HDR technique
and tuned against the PC game's own picture. They contain no console code or data. The light pools' lights and
shading are built at setup time from the player's own PC game files and are never distributed.

The setup kit downloads, into its own cache and checked against pinned SHA-256 hashes: Ghidra 12.1.3 (Apache 2.0,
National Security Agency), an OpenJDK 26.0.1 build (GPL-2.0 with the Classpath Exception) and, only when the Mac has
no Python 3.11 or later, a python-build-standalone CPython 3.12 build (PSF licence and its bundled licences).

Native PlayStation menu prompt adaptation (mods/core/nfsmw/frontend_pad.h) uses
structure, flag and resource-hash research from NFS-XtendedInput, commit
60958fc1928308023457e3ae94a2f3e79cf3c9b5. Its MIT notice follows:

